#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430779} :activity/id #uuid "ae35b94d-2e76-5fcd-b627-6a9afdd973c8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048621 :topic/id #uuid "af65e753-6f2e-5d89-8c0d-d3d5df2fc680" :topic/math-academy-id 4063} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430776 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281284 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "052b75ab-358d-5073-b438-8aabbcf16598" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278638} {:answer/id #uuid "21f1b1b1-4cbf-5384-abf1-011fb4b8e732" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278897} {:answer/id #uuid "62d28ecd-0906-545c-93e9-7da6ccef73dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279488} {:answer/id #uuid "84b65459-6cd3-563c-a465-2e40b3ffdf57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279785} {:answer/id #uuid "97292980-0d47-5775-9ecb-d399051901ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279929}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279929} :answer-field/id #uuid "f48d194b-9d36-54e9-be97-ae912aef2d30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281267}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "46773dfb-2950-5619-a7ba-c3559b8d83bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-161652" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1<{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<4\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?  \n\nI. $∂A\\cap A\\ne ∅$\n\nII. $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅$\n\nIII. $A=\\text{int}\\,A$" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/22/2273cd843c4bfaeaff10d49d3400d7661262673639a66034638d805eef5b1e47.png)\n\n   \n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/5e/5e473584ef5193b434ff6c138b08c4fbd9111a37252019143b1afb2d48c913c6.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. As we've seen, $A$ contains none of its boundary points, so we must have  $∂A\\cap A=∅.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅,$ where $∅$ denotes the empty set.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ contains none of its boundary points, we must have $A=\\text{int}\\,A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III  only.\""} :step/id #uuid "17d99301-dd16-5965-97b9-df38716417f5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430780}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430777 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278571 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06342551-0e88-5a6b-bd0d-60e7e94f92cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275532} {:answer/id #uuid "514cdd25-4769-5166-850c-a323f4ce90d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276204} {:answer/id #uuid "9472a39b-33fc-53d0-8cc5-e0e82c1a530a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276820} {:answer/id #uuid "a5d437f7-5a15-5e16-8d18-d6085aacba13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276973} {:answer/id #uuid "e15fd5f3-6d8f-5ca6-a7db-f5eec29b87ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277448}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275532} :answer-field/id #uuid "0377995e-0aa9-5059-b2c1-6fd8c5abf106" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277730}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2307ae9e-a249-5090-bfed-df0660f7b0eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-161601" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}<36\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ contains none of its boundary points.\n\nII. $A$ contains some (not all) of its boundary points.\n\nIII. $\\text{int}\\,A=A$" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/ff/ff1f735266f66879834e02ca7601e90e40dfa7929e5796d4a3f8cb4ff2828de7.png)\n\n  \n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nNotice that, in this case, the set $A$ contains none of its boundary points. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. As we've seen, $A$ contains none of its boundary points.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ contains none of its boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A=A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "73729323-9be1-5e40-94b8-3eddef70b4a7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430776}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430778 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193206 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "114232b4-2761-5665-9296-c185bd484017" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275631} {:answer/id #uuid "7880da2d-e957-5ca8-8e13-bcb627935744" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276569} {:answer/id #uuid "8c2c725f-185f-5f35-871e-79e343f1ed7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276749} {:answer/id #uuid "a5c9fbc8-b14c-5cbc-8b85-1785d478d974" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276972} {:answer/id #uuid "f330e754-04e9-557c-9d3e-6d2ab7556a6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277609}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277609} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8880f9f-c13c-59f9-8dce-1a13792353a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278077}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "68256c62-7faf-5747-9e8c-b2c67a0a95a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-161444" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 4\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅$\n\nII. $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A$\n\nIII. $∂A⊂A$" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/62/62c410c21e79e1bc688c1ff1a3440a207c62bcad42525bdc93fa2770b76d48bd.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/33/33a4738b642443c2535d347aa53ee2b7b80c800142c4844d94578769118ebfbc.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅,$ where $∅$ denotes the empty set.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. In this case, the set $A$ contains all of its boundary points, so we must have $∂A⊂A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} :step/id #uuid "992eb2be-3ddd-57bf-a76a-79f416d50e34" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430777}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430779 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240997 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3243b0c1-092b-5601-b0a7-3f3c3e1a11a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2<x<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238491} {:answer/id #uuid "3292f4f8-3340-5dcc-8508-318774f90133" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2<x<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238495} {:answer/id #uuid "99e00a77-24b1-59aa-8b87-eeed024a6ac6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\le x<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239391} {:answer/id #uuid "bc38a932-011d-51ce-8574-ad8d957e354d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0<x\\le 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239697} {:answer/id #uuid "ed989251-9ebe-5e5b-b96b-de3e3996d705" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0<x<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240146}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238495} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5caea72-1771-5754-bf55-8da480cdcb1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240708}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "47ef764e-19dd-5c12-b6a9-8ee51c79b104" :question/math-academy-id "q-105176" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-2\\le x<0,\\,0<y\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,\\bbox[5px,border: 1px solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}},\\,0<y<1\\,\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nThe set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/4f/4f65a735e53130f633635fdfc8cd6de6c4fa39ed1fb7913ba9679bb07e73a6a0.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/c7/c76cea9b33420bf6c2e03d8eecb105003ce8b4e619063cbf2b7fccd5bc596cb2.png)\n\nSo, in this case, the interior of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-2<x<0,\\,0<y<1\\,\\}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "cbd027d6-1f1e-5c1e-908b-e26efca43cff" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430778}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430780 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281303 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21a437c9-898a-560e-9410-f1b54bde1b49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-1\\le y\\le 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278896} {:answer/id #uuid "457a4dac-7b09-53f3-a0b2-fc26b6d17f3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-1\\le y<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279212} {:answer/id #uuid "6b30a6d1-cc0f-5b57-a13e-c4a4b0eff724" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279564} {:answer/id #uuid "be011cab-f94a-5242-9219-8918bad94e5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280262} {:answer/id #uuid "ca3cde79-af5e-5be0-aee1-d2ce3550f31a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-1<y<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280365}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280365} :answer-field/id #uuid "aec5203e-7431-5285-804f-2b85f96ff86c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281134}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "45bc0b9a-3391-5849-aa6e-c42fba251f8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-161702" :question/problem "![](images/84/84806884f843154c17c338eef0f50d83cae049aece675450e21e58dade6a9f34.png)\n\nConsider the set $A$ shown above. The interior of $A$ is \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,\\bbox[5px,border: 1px solid black]{\\phantom{AAAA}}\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nThe set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/ce/ce268d8b396ea63227734c1edd6b876bb0fe9755fa12a3b75be90c6ef4af5af3.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/84/84780a84406a3fd0e281737b979887e0de6b1e9fe39d440f379fbb2b84e80a3b.png)\n\nSo, in this case, the interior of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}-1<y<0\\}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e882a353-3705-56ae-8672-046cb8d6e7ed"}] :activity/title "Interiors and Boundaries of Sets" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430775}]}
